<figure class="delegated-flow" aria-labelledby="delegated-request">
  <figcaption>Illustrative workflow · Not a live transaction</figcaption>
  <div class="request-example">
    <span class="flow-label">A project manager asks</span>
    <blockquote id="delegated-request">“Add Tilya to Project Atlas through November 15.”</blockquote>
  </div>

  <section class="business-workflow" aria-labelledby="business-workflow-heading">
    <h3 id="business-workflow-heading">Business process workflow</h3>
    <ol class="business-steps" role="list">
      <li>
        <span class="step-index">01</span>
        <h4>Interpret</h4>
        <p>Identify the person, project and access duration.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-index">02</span>
        <h4>Authorize</h4>
        <p>Check delegated authority and resource policy. Obtain approval where required.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="execution-step">
        <span class="step-index">03</span>
        <h4>Grant access</h4>
        <p>Assign the Project Atlas role and provision access in the target application.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-index">04</span>
        <h4>Record</h4>
        <p>Log the approval and action, with requester and agent attribution.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-index">05</span>
        <h4>Review &amp; expire</h4>
        <p>Expire access on November 15. Keep access and connections reviewable and revocable.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="business-outcome"><strong>Business outcome</strong> Tilya has time-bound access to Project Atlas.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="under-the-hood" aria-labelledby="agent-execution-heading">
    <div class="execution-heading">
      <span class="flow-label">Under the hood · Step 03</span>
      <h3 id="agent-execution-heading">Agent execution path</h3>
      <p>How the approved change reaches the application.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="execution-explanation">
      <ol class="authority-steps" role="list" aria-label="Agent execution sequence">
        <li><strong>Requester + agent</strong><span>Carry identity context</span></li>
        <li><strong>Policy decision</strong><span>Check the permitted action</span></li>
        <li><strong>Scoped authority</strong><span>Limit access to the task</span></li>
        <li><strong>Governed connection</strong><span>Execute via API, MCP or workflow</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="authority-note">The agent’s authority to make the change is separate from Tilya’s access. Policy and resource controls enforce that boundary, not the AI model.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</figure>

<details class="enterprise-patterns">
  <summary>Enterprise patterns behind this solution</summary>
  <p>An agentic interface extends delegated administration; identity and integration controls still govern execution. These are example patterns, not public client case studies.</p>
  <dl>
    <div><dt>Healthcare</dt><dd>Provider administrators manage external provider users.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Media &amp; production</dt><dd>Production coordinators manage temporary project teams.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Enterprise</dt><dd>Managers and application owners manage employee or contractor access.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</details>

